#: R/reportcat.b.R
msgid ""
"These summaries are suitable for baseline characteristics tables, data "
"quality assessment, and descriptive analysis in clinical research."
msgstr ""

#: R/crosstable.b.R
msgid ""
"These variables are stored as numbers but take 6 or fewer distinct values, "
"so they are most likely encoded categories such as grade 1, 2, 3 rather than "
"measurements: %s. The table styles do not agree on how to handle them: "
"gtsummary summarises them as counts and percentages and tests them with a "
"chi-square or Fisher's exact test, finalfit does the same only when the "
"column has fewer than 5 distinct values counting missing as one of them, so "
"a four-value score with any missing data is treated as continuous there, and "
"arsenal, NEJM, Lancet and Hmisc treat any numeric column as continuous and "
"test the codes with ANOVA or Kruskal-Wallis. The p-value for these variables "
"can therefore change when you switch style, with nothing else changed. To "
"get the same handling in every style, set their measure type to Nominal or "
"Ordinal in the data; leave them Continuous if they really are measurements."
msgstr ""

#: R/crosstable.b.R
msgid ""
"These variables could not be matched to a column in the data: {vars} - so no "
"rows are shown for them and they are not included in any test. This usually "
"happens when a column was renamed, removed or re-typed after it was "
"selected. Re-select them in the Variables box, or drop them from the "
"selection to clear this message."
msgstr ""

#: R/crosstable.b.R
msgid ""
"These variables form a two-by-two table with the grouping variable: %s. With "
"Chi-square selected, the finalfit style applies Yates' continuity correction "
"to them while the arsenal and gtsummary styles report the uncorrected "
"Pearson chi-square. The corrected p-value is never the smaller of the two, "
"so a two-by-two p-value can move to the other side of 0.05 when nothing but "
"the style is changed. Fisher's exact test does not involve this choice."
msgstr ""

#: R/vartree.b.R
